course: dictionary
lesson: 12243
title: Words that stand out
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Conspicuous means very easy to see or notice. Something conspicuous stands out from everything around it. «Her yellow raincoat was conspicuous in the grey crowd.» Yellow is bright against grey, so you spot it at once. Use the context: what would fit here? Try "easy to see" in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Bright clothes are good for walking near roads, because they make you conspicuous to drivers. «Wear a light jacket so drivers can see you on dark evenings.» The clue is "so drivers can see you": the jacket helps you stand out.
explain: The word also appears in phrases. «The teacher noticed that Sam was conspicuous by his absence.» Sam was not there, and that was what people noticed. So "conspicuous by its absence" means noticeable because something is missing.
fact: Conspicuous means very easy to see or notice; it stands out.
fact: «Conspicuous» is an adjective, and «conspicuously» is the adverb: «She was dressed conspicuously in orange.»
fact: The comparative forms are «more conspicuous» and «most conspicuous».
fact: In stories, a conspicuous detail, such as muddy footprints, can be an important clue.
fact: Phrases include "make yourself conspicuous", "conspicuous by its absence" and "conspicuous spending" (spending that is very easy to notice).
exercise: «Her yellow raincoat was conspicuous in the grey crowd.» What does conspicuous mean? | Hidden behind other people | Very wet and cold | *Easy to see and standing out | Quiet and soft
exercise: «Wear bright clothes so drivers can see you.» Why does bright clothing make you conspicuous? | It is the cheapest kind of clothing | It makes you invisible in the dark | *It stands out, so drivers notice you | It keeps you warm in winter
exercise: Sam was missing from the class photo. Which phrase means noticeable because something is missing? | Conspicuous by its colour | *Conspicuous by its absence | Conspicuously happy | Conspicuous in the sun
test: «The muddy footprints by the back door were a conspicuous clue.» What does conspicuous suggest here? | Boring and not worth reading about | Hidden under the doormat | *Easy to notice and important | Ancient and very old
test: She was dressed ___ in bright orange. Which word fits the gap? | *conspicuously | consciously | conspicuous | conspire
exam: Which sentence uses conspicuous correctly? | *The bright red bus was conspicuous on the grey street | Her whisper was conspicuous as a shout in a hall full of noise | The sleeping cat was conspicuous under the blanket, hidden from sight | He crept quietly in the dark so that he was conspicuous to everyone
